[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) said the reports about dates were one issue with one fix queued. Read
by cause instead of by subject line, the same reports hold more than one problem, and the
parser fix closed only the first.

<Callout>
Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: <code>parser</code> fixed
(<code>fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales</code>), <code>hint</code> won't fix (the
booking-v3 form is retired). The one-day moves were not part of that update.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} {issues} {oneDayMove} />

Counted the desk's way: once per report id (the ticket export repeats some reports across its
pages), placed by the tenant's locale and booking form (`causes.ts`), not by intake's tag,
which guesses from the reporter's browser and is wrong in both directions.

## The problems

**`parser` — the shared parser read every date month first.** Every tenant on file writes the
day first. A date with both parts at 12 or below came out with day and month swapped
("7 April went out on 4 July"), at every tenant and through every channel. Fixed with #4471.

**`hint` — booking-v3's form told people to type the month first.** Its date field printed
`MM/DD/YYYY`, so people typed month first, and the form read the date the tenant's way, day
first. Only reports through the form at tenants on booking-v3 (Alder Physio, Copper Studio,
Pale Harbour). The symptom is the same swap, so subject lines merged it into #4471, but the
parser fix does not touch it. Won't fix: the form is retired.

**One-day moves — the date moves by one day, not a swap.** The desk already noted these
(`desk-notes.json`) and counts them under `parser` by tenant: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday.
Client in Toronto got Monday.", "Every booking in June is one day off", an invite that arrived
before the booking was made. That reads as a day boundary between time zones, which neither
fix changes, so it has its own ticket, still open.

## Why they ended up in one ticket

Merging by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) puts every
"wrong date" report in one place, whatever the cause. The cause table on #4471 already showed
the split: it marked `hint` as "not this ticket — no ticket of its own" and footnoted the
one-day moves under `parser`. Nobody filed them.

## What the desk does now

1. New swapped-date reports after 5 October are not #4471: check the tenant and form first.
2. Reports where the date moved by one day go to the one-day-move ticket.
3. When a big ticket's cause table shows more than one cause, file one ticket per cause.

